<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matthias Corvinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1443">1443</a>–<a href="/years/1490">1490</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Matthias I of Hungary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matthias Corvinus, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>, was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1458. He retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1463 and organised the Banate of Jajce, built a standing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> force known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-army-of-hungary">Black Army</a> and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a>, winning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1485. His court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, with a famous library, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-corviniana">Bibliotheca Corviniana</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus.jpg" alt="Matthias Corvinus" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea Mantegna · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_Mantegna_-_King_Matthias_Corvinus_of_Hungary.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
